export const DEEP_LEADERSHIP: DeepItem[] = [
  ["a charge nurse allocating four clients among staff", "an experienced RN, a new graduate, and an assistant to cover post-op, stable chronic, and unstable clients", "match the unstable client to the experienced RN and stable, predictable care to the assistant", "I will tell the charge nurse immediately if my client seems to be getting worse", "every client receives care matched to the caregiver's competence and scope", "Delegation law rests on predictability: assistive personnel take stable clients with expected outcomes and routine tasks (hygiene, vitals on stable clients, ambulation); licensed practical/enrolled nurses add medication administration and focused care of stable clients; registered nurses hold everything unstable, first-time, or requiring judgement — assessment, teaching, evaluation, IV push drugs, care planning. The five rights of delegation: right task, right circumstance, right person, right direction, right supervision. Accountability never delegates — the RN remains answerable for the outcome of every delegated task."],
  ["a nurse handed a verbal order during a resuscitation", "a rapid string of drug orders called out during the emergency", "repeat back each order aloud with dose and route before administering", "I understand the team repeats orders aloud to prevent mistakes under pressure", "all emergency medications are given accurately and documented after the event", "Closed-loop communication is the resuscitation safety net: the order is stated, repeated back verbatim by the receiver, and confirmed by the sender before execution — catching mishears of sound-alike drugs and misplaced decimal points when adrenaline (the hormone and the atmosphere) is highest. Verbal orders are legitimate ONLY in emergencies and sterile procedures; they are documented and countersigned as soon as the event permits. A designated recorder timestamps drugs and doses in real time because memory reconstruction after a resuscitation is demonstrably unreliable."],
  ["a client asking the nurse detailed questions the surgeon has not yet answered", "signed consent present but the client cannot describe risks or alternatives", "pause the preparation and inform the surgeon — informed consent is incomplete", "I know I can ask my surgeon questions and change my mind before the operation", "the client proceeds to surgery able to explain the procedure, risks, and alternatives", "The nurse's consent role is precise: witnessing the signature and advocating — verifying the client's understanding — while the DUTY to inform (procedure, risks, benefits, alternatives, right to refuse) belongs to the practitioner performing the procedure and cannot be delegated to nursing. A signature without understanding is legally void consent. Consent also requires capacity and voluntariness; sedative premedication given before signing invalidates it. The nurse who proceeds despite recognising a consent gap shares the liability — stopping the line is the professionally protected action."],
  ["a family demanding information about an adult client's diagnosis by telephone", "a caller claiming to be a relative requesting clinical details", "verify identity and the client's documented consent before disclosing anything", "I will choose a password so only the people I name can receive my information", "the client's confidentiality is maintained with information flowing only as consented", "Confidentiality survives telephone pressure through systems, not judgement calls: agreed passwords, documented consent lists, and a default of disclosing nothing to unverified callers — social engineering attacks on health information are common and convincing. Exceptions to confidentiality are narrow and legally defined: court orders, mandatory notifications (specified infectious diseases, suspected abuse), and genuine imminent-harm situations. Even then, disclosure is minimal-necessary. Corridor conversations, shared computers left open, and social media are the everyday breach routes the exam pairs with this principle."],
  ["a nurse asked to float to a specialised unit without orientation", "an assignment including clients on equipment the nurse has never used", "accept the stable general clients and decline unfamiliar specialised tasks, informing the coordinator", "I will always say clearly what I am and am not competent to take on", "float staffing maintains safety with tasks matched to demonstrated competence", "Floating obligations are two-way: the nurse cannot abandon the shift (walking out after accepting report is abandonment), but accepting tasks beyond competence makes the nurse liable for foreseeable harm — 'I was told to' is not a defence in professional law. The safe pathway is differential acceptance: take what general competence covers, decline what it does not, put the objection and the response in writing, and escalate through the staffing chain. Employers hold parallel duties: orientation to the unit, buddy allocation, and matching assignments to declared competencies."],
  ["two clients deteriorating simultaneously at opposite ends of the ward", "one client with new chest pain, another with a blocked catheter and distress", "attend the chest pain first and delegate or delay the catheter — threat to life outranks discomfort", "I understand the sickest person is seen first even when I am uncomfortable", "both clients are managed safely in order of clinical risk", "Simultaneous demand is triaged by survivability physiology: airway before breathing before circulation, unstable before stable, acute-and-unexpected before chronic-and-predictable. Chest pain is potential myocardial ischaemia — minutes of muscle; a blocked catheter is genuinely distressing but kills nobody in the next ten minutes and is delegable. The exam variant reverses the framing: 'which client can be seen LAST' or 'which is safe to delegate' — same hierarchy, inverted question. Documented reassessment of the deferred client closes the loop."],
  ["a nurse discovering a colleague documenting care that was not given", "vital signs charted for a client who was off the ward at that time", "report the falsification to the manager — fraudulent documentation endangers clients", "I understand my records must reflect exactly what happened and when", "documentation across the team is accurate, timely, and trustworthy", "Falsified records corrupt the clinical dataset every subsequent decision relies on: a fabricated 'normal' vital sign can mask deterioration until it is irreversible, and in law, care not documented was not given — while care documented but not given is fraud. Correct process for errors: single line through, initial, date, and the correction alongside — never obliteration, which reads as concealment. Late entries are labelled as such. Reporting a colleague's falsification is a professional duty; witnessing silently makes the witness complicit in the ongoing risk."],
  ["a ward receiving multiple casualties from a bus crash", "walking wounded, a silent client with sucking chest wound, and one without pulse or breathing", "triage by survivability: treat the chest wound first under mass-casualty rules", "I understand in a disaster the team helps those most likely to survive first", "casualties are sorted and treated according to triage category with resources used effectively", "Mass-casualty triage inverts everyday ethics: resources go to the greatest good for the greatest number, so the pulseless client — who would receive full resuscitation on an ordinary shift — is categorised expectant (black) while the salvageable critical client (sucking chest wound, red) takes priority. The walking wounded (green) wait. Quietness is ominous: the silent client may be too hypoxic to call out, while the loudest usually have airway enough to shout. Triage is dynamic — categories are reassessed as clients evolve and resources change."],
  ["a client wishing to leave against medical advice mid-treatment", "a capacitated client refusing further IV antibiotics and requesting discharge", "assess capacity, explain risks honestly, involve the provider, and never obstruct a capacitated adult", "I understand the risks explained to me and know I may return at any time", "the client departs informed with a safety-netted return plan, or chooses to stay", "Autonomy protects even choices clinicians consider unwise — the pivot is capacity: understanding, retaining, weighing the information, and communicating a decision. A capacitated adult cannot be detained by physical means, guilt, or threats ('you'll get no help if you leave' invalidates the process and constitutes coercion). The nurse's duties are procedural: risk explanation in plain language, provider notification, AMA documentation of exactly what was explained, removal of IV access for safety, medication and follow-up provision, and an explicit welcome to return — punishment discharge planning is unethical and dangerous."],
  ["a newly qualified nurse making a near-miss medication error caught at the bedside", "a wrong-client error stopped by the final identity check", "report the near miss through the incident system so the system weakness is analysed", "I will keep checking my armband every time, because that check just protected me", "near misses are reported and analysed with system fixes reducing repeat risk", "Near misses are free lessons: the same latent system failures (look-alike names, interrupted checks, unclear labelling) produce both the caught error and the eventual catastrophe — analysing the near miss fixes the trajectory before harm occurs. Just Culture separates human error (console, redesign the system), at-risk behaviour (coach), and reckless conduct (discipline): punishing honest error teaches concealment, and concealed error is the most dangerous kind. The bedside identity check that caught this error is the last of the Swiss-cheese layers — the report is what repairs the holes upstream."],
];

export const DEEP_HEALTHPROMO: DeepItem[] = [
  ["a client with newly diagnosed hypertension resisting medication", "a blood pressure of 168/104 with the client feeling completely well", "teach that hypertension damages silently and agree a monitoring plan the client owns", "I will take my tablets daily even though I feel fine, because damage happens silently", "blood pressure reaches target with the client self-monitoring at home", "Hypertension's danger is precisely its silence: years of asymptomatic pressure remodel arteries (atherosclerosis), thicken the left ventricle, shred retinal vessels, and scar glomeruli — the first 'symptom' is often the stroke itself. Feeling well is therefore irrelevant data, and that counterintuitive fact is the core teaching challenge. Adherence strategies that work: linking doses to existing habits, home self-monitoring (which makes the invisible visible), simplifying to once-daily regimens, and addressing side effects honestly since a client who feels worse ON treatment than off will quietly stop."],
  ["a client with a strong family history of type 2 diabetes", "an HbA1c of 6.2% placing the client in the prediabetic range", "enrol the client in structured lifestyle change — diet, activity, and weight targets", "I will aim to lose 5-7% of my weight and walk briskly 150 minutes weekly", "follow-up HbA1c returns below the prediabetic threshold", "Prediabetes is the last freely reversible station: landmark prevention trials showed structured lifestyle change (5-7% weight loss, 150 minutes weekly moderate activity) cuts progression to diabetes by 58% — outperforming metformin. Mechanistically, exercising muscle takes up glucose through insulin-INDEPENDENT transporters, directly relieving insulin resistance, and visceral fat loss removes the inflammatory driver. Screening matters because beta-cell function is already declining silently during prediabetes; waiting for symptoms means half the insulin-producing capacity is gone at diagnosis."],
  ["a mother hesitant about childhood immunisation", "a delayed vaccine schedule and circulating measles in the district", "listen to specific concerns respectfully and provide clear risk-benefit information", "I understand the diseases prevented are far more dangerous than the vaccines", "the child is fully immunised on the catch-up schedule", "Vaccine hesitancy is a spectrum, not a single position — effective response starts with motivational listening to the SPECIFIC concern (safety, number of injections, religious question, misinformation) rather than a lecture; confrontation entrenches resistance. Measles is the canary: the most transmissible common pathogen needing ~95% coverage for herd immunity, so it re-emerges first wherever coverage dips. Herd immunity framing recruits altruism — vaccination protects the immunocompromised child in the same classroom who cannot be vaccinated. Presumptive framing ('today's vaccines are due') outperforms open framing in uptake."],
  ["a 55-year-old smoker at a screening clinic", "a 30-pack-year history with a chronic morning cough", "offer combined cessation support — behavioural counselling plus pharmacotherapy — and appropriate screening", "I will set a quit date and use the patches and support programme together", "the client sustains cessation past six months with improving respiratory symptoms", "Combination treatment doubles-to-triples quit rates versus willpower alone: pharmacotherapy (nicotine replacement, varenicline, bupropion) blunts withdrawal biology while behavioural support restructures the habit architecture — cues, routines, substitute behaviours. The benefit timeline is powerful teaching: carbon monoxide clears in days, ciliary function recovers in months, excess cardiac risk halves within a year, and lung cancer risk halves by ten years. Relapse is reframed as a learning event within the change cycle, not failure — most successful quitters needed several attempts."],
  ["a client asking about cervical cancer prevention for her daughters", "two daughters aged 10 and 12 not yet vaccinated against HPV", "recommend HPV vaccination now — it works best before exposure begins", "my daughters will complete the HPV course and still attend screening when older", "both daughters are vaccinated with screening understood for adulthood", "HPV vaccination is cancer prevention in the most literal sense: types 16 and 18 cause ~70% of cervical cancers, and the vaccine's efficacy approaches 100% — but only against types not yet acquired, which is why the schedule targets ages 9-14, before sexual debut, where two doses also suffice immunologically. Population data already show >85% falls in cervical disease among early-vaccinated cohorts. Screening remains necessary because the vaccine does not cover every oncogenic type. Framing it as anti-cancer rather than anything to do with sexual behaviour dissolves most parental hesitancy."],
  ["an overweight client with knee osteoarthritis avoiding all activity", "activity avoidance due to pain with progressive deconditioning", "prescribe low-impact exercise — water-based activity, cycling — and graded goals", "I will exercise in the pool where my joints are supported, three times weekly", "pain scores fall as activity tolerance and joint function improve", "The intuition 'worn joints need rest' is backwards: cartilage has no blood supply and is nourished by synovial fluid pumped through it by cyclical loading — movement literally feeds the joint. Muscle strengthening (especially quadriceps) offloads the knee mechanically, and each kilogram of weight lost removes roughly four kilograms of knee-joint load per step. Water immersion cuts joint loading dramatically while providing resistance for strengthening. Rest-driven deconditioning creates the true downward spiral: weaker muscles, stiffer joints, more pain, less activity."],
  ["a client recovering from a myocardial infarction preparing for discharge", "fear of any exertion including sexual activity and stair climbing", "refer to cardiac rehabilitation and address activity fears with concrete milestones", "I will attend rehab and progress my activity by the staged plan I was given", "the client completes rehabilitation returning to full daily activity", "Cardiac rehabilitation cuts mortality by ~25% yet remains massively under-attended — fear is the barrier, and concrete milestones are its antidote. Useful equivalences: climbing two flights of stairs comfortably approximates the metabolic demand of sexual activity (3-5 METs), giving clients a private self-test for a question they rarely ask aloud. Exercise builds coronary collateral circulation and improves endothelial function; the damaged heart strengthens through progressive overload exactly as skeletal muscle does. Depression after MI doubles mortality and is screened for actively — it masquerades as 'lack of motivation.'"],
  ["an older client at a falls-prevention review", "three near-falls this year, four sedating medications, and poor lighting at home", "conduct multifactorial risk reduction: medication review, strength and balance training, home hazards", "I will do my balance exercises daily and use the night lights we installed", "no falls occur with improved confidence and mobility at follow-up", "Falls are never 'just tripping' — they are the final common pathway of interacting risks: sarcopenia, polypharmacy (sedatives, antihypertensives, anticholinergics), postural hypotension, vision decline and environmental hazards. Multifactorial intervention works precisely because single fixes fail; strength-and-balance training (Tai Chi, Otago) has the strongest single-component evidence, cutting falls ~30%. The physiology compounds: one fall breeds fear, fear breeds restriction, restriction accelerates the muscle loss that caused the fall. A fall in an older adult also flags fracture risk — bone health assessment belongs in every falls review."],
  ["a client with harmful alcohol use identified by screening", "drinking above low-risk limits daily with morning eye-openers", "deliver a brief intervention using motivational interviewing and offer referral", "I have set a reduction plan with review dates and know where to get support", "alcohol intake falls with engagement in ongoing support", "Screening and brief intervention is among the highest-yield preventive acts in primary care: 5-15 minutes of structured, non-judgemental feedback measurably reduces hazardous drinking. Motivational interviewing works with ambivalence rather than against it — arguing FOR change positions the client to argue against it; instead, open questions elicit the client's own change talk (people believe their own words first). The morning 'eye-opener' flags physical dependence: such clients must NOT stop abruptly without medical support, because unmanaged alcohol withdrawal seizures and delirium tremens kill."],
  ["a community with rising teenage pregnancy rates", "school-leavers with little access to accurate reproductive health information", "implement comprehensive, age-appropriate education and accessible youth-friendly services", "I know where to get confidential advice and contraception in my community", "teenage pregnancy rates decline with increased service uptake", "The evidence is consistent and counterintuitive to some communities: comprehensive sexuality education DELAYS sexual debut and increases protection use, while abstinence-only approaches show no effect on timing but correlate with unprotected first intercourse. Youth-friendly means confidential, non-judgemental, affordable and geographically reachable — adolescents avoid services where they expect moralising or parental disclosure. Teenage pregnancy compounds across generations: curtailed education, economic disadvantage, higher obstetric risk (eclampsia, obstructed labour in still-growing pelvises). Community and religious leader engagement determines programme survival."],
];

export const DEEP_RISKREDUCTION: DeepItem[] = [
  ["a client scheduled for surgery who takes several herbal supplements", "garlic, ginkgo, and fish oil listed on the pre-operative checklist", "report the supplements — several increase bleeding and must be stopped before surgery", "I will stop the listed supplements as instructed and bring every bottle to pre-assessment", "surgery proceeds without abnormal bleeding or anaesthetic interactions", "The three Gs — garlic, ginkgo, ginger — plus fish oil and vitamin E all inhibit platelet aggregation, compounding surgical bleeding risk; St John's wort induces liver enzymes that alter anaesthetic and warfarin metabolism, and it cannot be stopped abruptly. Most guidance stops such supplements 1-2 weeks pre-operatively. The deeper lesson is the medication history itself: half of supplement users never volunteer them because 'natural' is assumed safe and clinicians rarely ask. The pre-operative checklist question exists because these interactions caused real deaths."],
  ["a client with a plaster cast on a fresh forearm fracture", "pain out of proportion, worse on passive finger stretch, unrelieved by analgesia", "treat as compartment syndrome: elevate no higher than the heart and call the provider urgently for cast splitting", "I will seek help immediately for severe pain, numbness, or colour change in my fingers", "the limb remains perfused with intact sensation and movement throughout casting", "Compartment syndrome is pressure physiology: bleeding and swelling inside an inelastic fascial (and plaster) envelope collapse capillary perfusion long before pulses disappear — a present pulse NEVER excludes it. Pain out of proportion and pain on passive stretch are the earliest signs; paralysis and pulselessness are the tombstones. Muscle necrosis begins around 4-6 hours. Elevation stays AT heart level: higher drops arterial inflow into the already-starved compartment. Definitive treatment is fasciotomy; splitting the cast buys time. Volkmann's contracture is the permanent deformity of the missed case."],
  ["a client one day after upper abdominal surgery refusing to mobilise", "shallow guarded breathing, absent cough, and reluctance to move due to pain", "control pain adequately FIRST, then mobilise, breathe deeply, and use the spirometer", "I will press my pillow against my wound to splint it when I cough and walk", "recovery proceeds without chest infection, clots, or ileus", "Post-operative complications are a cascade with pain at the top: uncontrolled pain leads to shallow breathing and no cough, then atelectasis then pneumonia; immobility leads to venous stasis then thromboembolism, plus ileus, pressure injury and deconditioning. Analgesia is therefore not comfort but prophylaxis — the client who 'toughs it out' is choosing pneumonia. Wound splinting with a pillow opposes incisional stress during cough, making effective airway clearance tolerable. Enhanced recovery protocols compress all of this: early feeding, early walking, multimodal opioid-sparing analgesia — each element defending against a specific cascade branch."],
  ["a client receiving contrast for a CT scan with a shellfish allergy question raised", "anxiety about contrast because of a shellfish allergy history", "clarify the actual risk factors: prior contrast reactions, asthma, and atopy — not shellfish specifically", "I will report itching, swelling, or breathlessness during the scan immediately", "imaging is completed safely with appropriate observation afterwards", "The shellfish-iodine-contrast chain is a durable medical myth: shellfish allergy is to tropomyosin proteins, not iodine — no one is allergic to iodine, an essential element in thyroid hormone. Real risk stratifiers are previous contrast reactions (highest), asthma, and significant atopy; these may warrant premedication or alternative imaging. Contrast reactions are mostly anaphylactoid (direct mast-cell degranulation, no prior sensitisation needed), so they can occur on first exposure. Separate risk entirely: contrast-induced nephropathy — screened by renal function, mitigated by hydration, and the reason metformin is held."],
  ["a client on bed rest with a swollen, tender calf awaiting Doppler", "unilateral calf swelling 3 cm greater than the other side with warmth", "keep the client resting, never massage the limb, and expedite diagnostic imaging", "I will not rub my leg and will report any sudden breathlessness at once", "the thrombus is treated with no embolic event and full limb recovery", "The 3-cm circumference difference is a formal Wells criterion for DVT probability. The no-massage rule is mechanical: a fresh thrombus is friable, loosely adherent fibrin and red cells — external pressure can shear it free to embolise the pulmonary circulation, converting a leg problem into an arrest call. Sudden dyspnoea, pleuritic pain or haemoptysis in any client with a known DVT is a pulmonary embolism until proven otherwise. Anticoagulation does not dissolve the existing clot; it halts propagation while endogenous fibrinolysis works."],
  ["a client with a tracheostomy showing copious thick secretions", "thick secretions the client cannot clear with a weak cough", "provide humidification, hydration, and suction using sterile technique only as needed", "family will learn suctioning with the exact steps demonstrated before discharge", "the airway stays clear with secretions thin and manageable", "A tracheostomy bypasses the nose — the body's humidifier, filter and warmer — so unconditioned air desiccates secretions into airway-obstructing plugs; humidification is therefore life-safety, not comfort. Suction rules encode oxygen physiology: suction only on withdrawal, maximum 10-15 seconds per pass (the client receives no air during suction), pre-oxygenate, and use a catheter no larger than half the tube's diameter to allow airflow around it. Routine scheduled suctioning traumatises mucosa and provokes MORE secretions; suction responds to assessment findings — audible secretions, desaturation, visible distress."],
  ["a client admitted with acute kidney injury after starting several new medicines", "creatinine doubling within days of starting an NSAID, ACE inhibitor, and diuretic", "hold the nephrotoxic combination and manage fluids per prescription", "I will avoid ibuprofen-type painkillers unless my kidney doctor approves them", "kidney function returns to baseline with a revised, kidney-safe regimen", "This is the 'triple whammy': each drug attacks one leg of glomerular filtration's tripod — the NSAID blocks prostaglandin-mediated AFFERENT arteriolar dilation (inflow), the ACE inhibitor blocks angiotensin-mediated EFFERENT constriction (back-pressure), and the diuretic depletes circulating volume. Together they can collapse filtration pressure within days, especially in elderly, dehydrated or heart-failure clients. AKI itself is staged by creatinine rise and urine output fall. Recovery hinges on rapid recognition; the exam expects the combination named as the cause, not any single agent."],
  ["a client post-lumbar puncture reporting a positional headache", "severe frontal headache on sitting that vanishes when lying flat", "keep the client flat, encourage fluids and caffeine, and report persistence for possible blood patch", "I will lie flat as advised and drink well; I will call if the headache continues tomorrow", "the headache resolves without further intervention", "The positional signature IS the diagnosis: CSF leaking through the dural puncture lowers intracranial pressure, so upright posture lets the brain sag on its pain-sensitive anchoring structures — flat positioning re-floats it. CSF regenerates at ~500 mL/day, usually outpacing the leak within days. Caffeine constricts the compensatorily dilated intracranial vessels. The definitive treatment, an epidural blood patch, injects the client's own blood to clot over the dural hole — ~90% effective. Distinguish this benign (if miserable) headache from post-procedure red flags: fever, neck stiffness and photophobia suggest iatrogenic meningitis instead."],
  ["a client with atrial fibrillation newly started on warfarin", "a plan for regular INR monitoring alongside multiple drug and food interactions", "establish INR monitoring, a consistent diet, and an interaction check for every new medicine", "I will keep leafy-green intake steady, avoid alcohol binges, and check before any new tablet", "the INR remains 2.0-3.0 with no bleeding or embolic events", "Warfarin blocks vitamin K recycling, depleting clotting factors II, VII, IX and X over 3-5 days (hence bridging with heparin, which acts instantly). The INR window 2-3 is narrow: below it, stroke risk returns; above it, bleeding climbs steeply. Interactions are the management battlefield — antibiotics kill vitamin-K-producing gut flora, many drugs displace warfarin from albumin or alter its hepatic metabolism, and dietary vitamin K works AGAINST the drug, so the rule is consistency of greens, not avoidance. In atrial fibrillation, the fibrillating atrium clots in its appendage; warfarin prevents the embolic stroke that is AF's true danger."],
  ["a client recovering from a stroke with right-sided neglect", "food untouched on the right side of the tray and bumping into right-side objects", "approach and place items within the intact visual field while retraining scanning to the right", "family will remind gentle scanning to the affected side during meals and walks", "the client compensates effectively with safe navigation and complete meals", "Neglect is not blindness — it is an attention disorder from (usually right) parietal damage: the client's brain does not register that the affected side of space EXISTS, so they do not experience anything as missing, which is why insight is absent and safety risk is high. Early management is compensatory (essentials placed on the intact side); rehabilitation then deliberately retrains attention across the midline — scanning training, cueing, mirrors. Neglect predicts falls, burns and pedestrian danger better than weakness does. Homonymous hemianopia coexists commonly but differs: the field-cut client KNOWS to turn their head; the neglect client must be taught that the other side exists."],
];

export const DEEP_GERONTOLOGY: DeepItem[] = [
  ["an 82-year-old presenting 'off legs' with none of the classic infection signs", "new confusion and falls with a normal temperature but positive urine culture", "recognise atypical presentation of illness in old age and assess systematically", "family will report sudden confusion or falls as possible illness, not 'just ageing'", "the underlying infection is treated with the client returning to baseline function", "Ageing blunts every classic alarm: thermoregulation fails (serious infection with no fever), pain signalling dampens (silent myocardial infarction, painless abdomen in surgical emergencies), and homeostatic reserve narrows — so illness presents as the geriatric giants: falls, confusion, incontinence, 'off legs,' stopping eating. The clinical rule is inverted vigilance: ANY abrupt functional or cognitive change in an older person is organic illness until proven otherwise. Baseline function documented from family is the diagnostic gold — without it, delirium reads as dementia and reversible illness gets palliated."],
  ["a 78-year-old on eleven regular medications after multiple specialist reviews", "dizziness, two falls, and three drugs prescribed to treat other drugs' side effects", "initiate structured deprescribing with the prescriber using a recognised criteria tool", "I will bring every medicine to one nominated doctor who reviews the whole list", "the regimen is rationalised with symptoms improved and no further falls", "Polypharmacy compounds geometrically, not linearly — at ten drugs, interaction probability approaches certainty. The prescribing cascade is its engine: a side effect is misread as a new disease and treated with a new drug (antipsychotic causing parkinsonism treated with levodopa). Ageing pharmacology amplifies everything: renal clearance falls (but muscle-poor elders show deceptively normal creatinine), body fat rises so lipophilic drugs linger, and albumin falls so protein-bound drugs run free. Deprescribing is an active clinical skill — tools like STOPP/START formalise it — and one prescriber holding the whole list is the single best defence."],
  ["an 85-year-old hospitalised for pneumonia now on strict bed rest", "day-three deconditioning with new dependence in transfers", "mobilise early and daily — bed rest is a treatment with severe side effects in old age", "I will sit out for meals and walk the corridor with help every day", "the client discharges home at pre-admission mobility", "Older adults lose muscle mass up to ten times faster than the young during bed rest — up to 5% strength per day — and hospital-associated deconditioning, not the pneumonia, is what sends previously independent elders to care homes. The cascade: bed rest, then sarcopenia and orthostatic intolerance, then falls and dependence, then institutionalisation. 'Pyjama paralysis' campaigns exist because dress and routine signal function to both client and staff. Early mobilisation is as much a treatment as the antibiotic; every hospital day without walking is measurable independence lost."],
  ["a 79-year-old with worsening memory brought in by worried family", "gradual two-year memory decline now affecting finances and medication management", "screen cognition formally, exclude reversible causes, and refer for comprehensive assessment", "family will attend the memory clinic and set up powers of attorney while capacity allows", "the client receives diagnosis, support planning, and future-care decisions made in time", "The dementia workup exists to catch the mimics: hypothyroidism, B12 deficiency, depression ('pseudodementia' — profound but reversible cognitive slowing), normal-pressure hydrocephalus (gait-incontinence-cognition triad), and subdural haematoma are all treatable. True dementias differentiate by pattern: Alzheimer's erodes recent memory first; vascular dementia steps down abruptly with each infarct; Lewy body brings hallucinations, fluctuation and parkinsonism (and lethal antipsychotic sensitivity). Early diagnosis is not nihilism — it is the window for legal planning, driving decisions and treatment while the person can still choose."],
  ["an older widower eating one meal daily since his wife died", "a 6 kg weight loss in three months with an empty refrigerator", "screen nutrition formally and address the social, dental, and functional drivers of undernutrition", "I will join the community lunch club and use the delivered-meals service", "weight stabilises with improved energy and social re-engagement", "Undernutrition in elders hides behind 'normal ageing': anorexia of ageing (blunted hunger hormones), dentition and dysphagia, drugs suppressing appetite or taste, depression, poverty and — as here — the loss of the person who made eating social. Consequences cascade: sarcopenia, immune failure, pressure injuries, falls and fractures. Protein needs actually RISE in old age (1.0-1.2 g/kg) against intake that falls. Screening tools (MUST, MNA) quantify what 'he looks thin' misses; the loneliness on this man's plate is as pathological as any lesion, and the lunch club is a prescription."],
  ["an older client asking about advance care planning after a friend's ICU death", "no documented wishes and family members who disagree about 'what Dad would want'", "facilitate advance care planning conversations and document decisions while capacity is intact", "I have written my wishes, chosen my proxy, and told my family what matters to me", "future care follows the client's documented values without family conflict", "Advance care planning is a process, not a form: values conversations ('what does a good day look like? what would be worse than dying?') anchor the documents — advance directives, resuscitation decisions, and a chosen healthcare proxy who knows the reasoning, not just the conclusions. Done early, it prevents the ICU scenario where a divided family projects their own grief into 'what Dad would want' at 3 am. Capacity is decision-specific and fluctuates, so planning belongs in stable times. Evidence shows ACP increases goal-concordant care and reduces family PTSD after bereavement."],
  ["an older client with chronic insomnia requesting sleeping tablets", "nightly benzodiazepine requests with daytime napping and four coffees daily", "apply sleep hygiene and CBT-I first; avoid initiating benzodiazepines in older adults", "I will keep a fixed wake time, cut afternoon caffeine, and get morning daylight", "sleep consolidates without hypnotics and daytime function improves", "Benzodiazepines in elders trade minutes of sleep for major harms: fall and hip-fracture risk rises ~50%, cognition dulls, dependence establishes within weeks, and the drugs suppress the deep slow-wave sleep that makes sleep restorative — they sedate rather than truly sleep. Ageing physiologically advances and lightens sleep; naps and caffeine then dismantle night-time sleep pressure. CBT-I (stimulus control, sleep restriction, fixed wake time) outperforms hypnotics durably and is first-line. The paradox worth teaching: LESS time in bed initially produces MORE actual sleep."],
  ["an older client with stress incontinence embarrassed to leave home", "leaking with cough and laughter, managed with restricted drinking and isolation", "teach pelvic floor muscle training and correct the harmful fluid restriction", "I will do my pelvic exercises daily and keep drinking normally through the day", "continence improves with restored confidence and social activity", "Incontinence is never normal ageing — it is pathology with a type-specific fix, and mislabeling it 'just age' is why fewer than half of sufferers ever seek help. Stress incontinence (sphincter/pelvic floor weakness, leaks with pressure spikes) responds to pelvic floor training in up to 75% with correct sustained technique. Urge incontinence (detrusor overactivity) responds to bladder retraining. The intuitive self-treatment — drinking less — backfires: concentrated urine irritates the bladder and dehydration adds falls and confusion. Incontinence drives care-home admission more than dementia severity; treating it is independence medicine."],
  ["a family carer of a client with advanced dementia at breaking point", "carer weight loss, insomnia, and admissions of shouting at the client", "assess carer strain as a clinical priority and mobilise respite and support services", "I will use the day-centre respite twice weekly and join the carers' group", "the client remains safely at home with the carer's health recovering", "Carer collapse is the commonest reason dementia care at home fails — carer strain predicts institutionalisation better than the client's disease severity does. Chronic caregiving stress doubles depression rates and measurably raises carer mortality; the shouting disclosed here is a strain signal and an elder-abuse risk flag demanding support, not judgement. Respite is clinical treatment for the CARER, and carers typically resist it out of guilt — reframing respite as what keeps home care sustainable unlocks acceptance. Assessment tools (Zarit) quantify what devoted families hide."],
  ["an older client transferred between three care settings in one month", "duplicated prescriptions, a missed diagnosis, and conflicting discharge instructions", "perform structured medication reconciliation and a verified handover at every transition", "I will keep one updated list of my medicines and bring it to every appointment", "transitions occur without medication errors or missed follow-up", "Care transitions are the most dangerous moments in an older person's healthcare: half of hospitalised elders experience a medication discrepancy at discharge, and transition errors drive early readmissions. Reconciliation is a formal three-way comparison — what was taken before, what was given during, what is prescribed after — resolving every difference deliberately. Elders are uniquely exposed: multiple prescribers, long lists, cognitive load, and the assumption that 'someone else' checked. The client-held single list is the humble tool with outsized effect; the structured handover (SBAR) is its professional counterpart."],
];
